From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: Fix wrong physical address in
 Tx frag unmap

The three places in this file that unmap a Tx fragment's DMA mapping
all compute the address to unmap as virt_to_phys(frag), where frag is
a pointer to the skb_frag_t descriptor inside skb_shinfo(skb)->frags[]
-- kernel metadata describing the fragment, not the fragment's actual
page. The page was mapped at submission time with
skb_frag_dma_map(dev, frag, ...), which maps skb_frag_page(frag) (plus
its offset), an entirely different piece of memory than the
skb_frag_t struct itself. dma_unmap_page() has therefore always been
called with the wrong physical address for every scatter-gather
(TSO/GSO) Tx completion and every Tx DMA-mapping-failure cleanup path,
not just on the out-of-bounds frag_index values the previous patch
guards against.

This is not inert on this hardware. The qcom,ipq5332-ppe /
ethernet-dma devicetree node has no "dma-coherent" property, so the
kernel treats this device as non-coherent DMA. On the generic
dma-direct path, dma_unmap_page() on a non-coherent device calls
arch_sync_dma_for_cpu() regardless of direction, which performs a real
cache-maintenance operation (inval/clean) over [addr, addr+size) of
whatever physical address it's given. With the wrong address, that's a
PAGE_SIZE-aligned window of unrelated live memory -- exactly the kind
of load-dependent, garbage-fault-address corruption behind tonight's
two panics. The previous patch's bounds check (frag_index vs
nr_frags) is still correct and worth keeping, but it only prevents an
out-of-bounds *array read*; every in-bounds, perfectly normal
multi-fragment Tx completion was already hitting this same wrong-
address unmap before that patch and still does after it.

Fix all three sites to unmap virt_to_phys(skb_frag_address(frag))
instead of virt_to_phys(frag) -- skb_frag_address() resolves to the
fragment's actual page + offset, mirroring the existing
virt_to_phys(skb->data) pattern already used correctly elsewhere in
this file for the non-fragment (head) buffer. Also fix the unmap
length at each site from a hardcoded PAGE_SIZE to the fragment's own
skb_frag_size(frag)/buf_len, matching what was actually mapped;
unmapping a full page when only part of it was the DMA buffer risked
invalidating cached, not-yet-flushed CPU writes to whatever else
shared that page.

Compile-tested (clean module build). Not yet re-verified under load on
hardware as of this commit.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_tx.c
@@ -195,8 +195,18 @@
 				} else {
 					skb_frag_t *frag = &skb_shinfo(skb)->frags[frag_index];
 
-					dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(frag),
-						       PAGE_SIZE, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+					/* virt_to_phys(frag) is the address of the
+					 * skb_frag_t descriptor itself (skb_shinfo
+					 * metadata), not of the page it describes --
+					 * that page was mapped via skb_frag_dma_map()
+					 * at submission time. Unmapping the wrong
+					 * address triggers cache maintenance on
+					 * unrelated memory on this non-dma-coherent
+					 * device, corrupting whatever happens to sit
+					 * in that PAGE_SIZE window.
+					 */
+					dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(skb_frag_address(frag)),
+						       skb_frag_size(frag), DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 				}
 			}
 
@@ -342,7 +352,7 @@
 		if (unlikely(buf_len == 0))
 			continue;
 
-		dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(frag), PAGE_SIZE,
+		dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(skb_frag_address(frag)), buf_len,
 			       DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 	}
 }
@@ -531,8 +541,8 @@
 			if (unlikely(skb_frag_size(frag) == 0))
 				continue;
 
-			dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(frag),
-				       PAGE_SIZE, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
+			dma_unmap_page(dev, virt_to_phys(skb_frag_address(frag)),
+				       skb_frag_size(frag), DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 			cnt_sg_frag_list += 1;
 			if (cnt_sg_frag_list == num_sg_frag_list)
 				return;
